Part 1: The Toolbelt (Libraries and Imports)

    Before building the brain, the system must import tools created by other programmers.
    Each import statement brings a specific tool into the workspace.

    from fastapi import FastAPI: FastAPI is the main framework. It is the "building" where the AI lives. It allows
                                 the program to create URLs (web addresses) so the outside world can communicate with the Python code.

    from pydantic import BaseModel: Pydantic acts as the strict security guard at the front door. BaseModel is a tool used
                                    to create strict contracts. If a user tries to send data that does not match the contract,
                                    Pydantic rejects it automatically.

    import uvicorn: Uvicorn is the engine. While FastAPI builds the house, Uvicorn is the electricity that powers it,
                    keeping the server awake and listening to the internet 24/7.

    import os: The "Operating System" library. It allows the Python code to look outside of itself and read hidden
               files on the computer or server, specifically looking for secret passwords.

    import requests: The "telephone" library. Whenever this backend needs to talk to another server
                    (like Groq's AI or Tavily's search engine), it uses requests to dial their number and send a message over the internet.

    import re: Stands for "Regular Expressions." It is a text-surgeon tool. It is used to scan sentences and carve out
                very specific patterns (like removing all alphabet letters to leave only math numbers behind).

    from dotenv import load_dotenv: A tool that opens a hidden file named .env and loads the secret API keys into
                                    the system's memory so they are never written directly in the visible code.

    from datetime import datetime: The digital clock. It allows the system to know exactly what day and year it is,
                                    which is crucial when the AI searches the web for current events.

    from fastapi.middleware.cors import CORSMiddleware: CORS stands for "Cross-Origin Resource Sharing." By default,
                                                        web browsers block external websites from talking to servers they don't belong to.
                                                        This middleware is the "Guest Pass" that allows frontends (like a mobile app or a Streamlit website)
                                                        to legally enter the API.

    from supabase import create_client, Client: The official toolkit to connect to Supabase, which acts as the system's database (the filing cabinet).

    from huggingface_hub import InferenceClient: The bridge to Hugging Face, an AI platform. This tool is specifically
                                                 used as a "translator" to convert English words into mathematical numbers.

Part 2: Waking Up the Brain (Initialization)

    After importing the tools, the system must turn them on and connect them.

    load_dotenv(): This command executes the dotenv tool, pulling all secrets from the hidden .env file into the active memory.

    API_KEY & CLOUD_URL: These variables store the secret password to Groq's AI and the exact web address to reach it.

    url & key: These variables store the address and password for the Supabase database.

    supabase: Client = create_client(url, key): This creates a permanent, secure bridge to the Supabase database.
             The resulting bridge is stored in the supabase variable, which the rest of the code will use whenever it needs to open the filing cabinet.

    app = FastAPI(...): This creates the main server application and gives it a title ("Enterprise Swarm API").
                        The app variable is the heart of the system.

    app.add_middleware(...): This attaches the CORS tool to the app. By setting allow_origins=["*"], the system explicitly
                             tells the internet: "I will accept requests from any website or mobile app."

    active_sessions = {}: This creates an empty dictionary (a list of key-value pairs). It acts as the server's short-term RAM.
                          When users chat, their ongoing conversation history is temporarily stored here so the server can remember
                          what was said five minutes ago without searching the database every single time.

Part 3: The Blueprints (Pydantic Data Contracts)

    When a message arrives at the server, it comes as a raw, messy chunk of text. The system uses Pydantic classes to force
    that text into a strict, perfectly organized box.

    1. class UserRequest(BaseModel)
        This defines the exact shape of a request coming in from a user who wants to chat.

        user_id: str: The system demands a string of text representing the unique ID of the person asking
                      (e.g., "discord_123"). This ensures the system knows exactly whose memory to open.

        prompt: str: The actual question the user typed.

        Why we use it: Later in the code, when the system types request.prompt, it is using this blueprint.
                       The .prompt part tells Python to reach inside the neatly packed UserRequest box and pull out only the user's question.

    2. class SwarmResponse(BaseModel)
    This defines the exact shape of the package the server will send back to the user.

        manager_routing: str: A string telling the frontend which departments the AI used (e.g., "RAG" or "WEB").

        final_answer: str: The actual English response generated by the AI.

    3. class DocumentUpload(BaseModel)
        This defines the strict rules for uploading new knowledge to the system's brain.

        admin_password: str: A secret password. If the user doesn't provide this, the door stays locked.

        user_id: str: The ID of the user who owns this document. This property is what makes the system secure;
                      it ties the document to one specific person so others cannot read it.

        content: str: The text data being uploaded.

Part 4: The Core AI Functions (The Specialized Workers)

    The system has several standalone helper functions. These are like highly specialized workers who sit at their desks
    waiting for the main program to call their names.

    def get_embedding(text):

        Usage: This function translates English into Math.

        How it works: It takes a text string, grabs the Hugging Face API key, and uses the InferenceClient to send that text
                      to an AI model named BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5.

        What is happening: The Hugging Face AI reads the meaning of the text and converts it into a "Vector"—a massive array
                            of 384 numbers (like [0.012, -0.453, 0.992...]).

        Why we use it: Databases are bad at understanding English, but they are incredibly fast at calculating the distance
                        between numbers. By turning text into math, the database can later find documents that have a similar
                        meaning to the user's question. The function uses hasattr(vector, "tolist") and isinstance(vector, list)
                         to ensure the math array is perfectly flattened before returning it.

    def get_manager_decision(user_text):

        Usage: This is the Routing Orchestrator.

        How it works: It holds a strict orchestrator_prompt that tells the Groq Llama 3.1 AI model to act as a manager.
                      It sends the user's question to Groq using requests.post.

        What is happening: The AI reads the question and outputs a single word (the routing decision). If the question is
                           about the CEO, it outputs "RAG". If it is about the weather, it outputs "WEB".

    def get_search_query(user_text):

        Usage: The Micro Agent for web searches.

        How it works: It uses the datetime library to inject today's exact date into a prompt. It sends the prompt to Groq.

        What is happening: It looks at a messy human question like "Can you tell me who won the cricket match yesterday?"
                           and translates it into a precise, Google-friendly search string like "Cricket match winner May 15 2026".
                           If the question is not about the web, it outputs the word "NONE".

    def send_to_cloud_ai(history_list):

        Usage: The main communicator. It is the only function responsible for generating final conversational answers.

        How it works: It takes the entire history_list (everything the user and the AI have said to each other) and sends it to Groq.

        The Safety Shield: When the data comes back (data = response.json()), the function checks if "choices" in data:.
                            Sometimes, cloud APIs crash or run out of memory. If Groq crashes, it won't send the "choices" block.
                            Instead of letting the entire Python server crash, this if/else statement catches the error gracefully
                            and returns a polite apology message to the user.

    def compress_memory(history_list):

        Usage: The background janitor.

        How it works: AI models have a limit to how much text they can read at once. If a user chats for a long time,
                      the memory gets too heavy. This function takes the long conversation, adds a strict prompt telling
                      the AI to create a "Running Fact Sheet," and passes it to send_to_cloud_ai. It shrinks pages of
                      conversation into a neat, bulleted summary.

    def calculate_math(expression):

        Usage: Evaluates mathematical equations.

        How it works: It uses Python's native eval() method. If it receives the string "50 / 2", eval() processes
                        the string as actual code and returns the integer 25.

    def perform_web_search(query):

        Usage: Connects to the live internet.

        How it works: It packages the search query into a JSON payload and uses requests.post to send it to the Tavily API.

        What is happening: Tavily is an AI search engine. It crawls the internet, finds the top 3 articles, and returns them.
                           The function then uses a for loop (for res in results:) to stitch the titles and text snippets together
                            into one long string of readable context.

Part 5: The API Endpoints (The Main Front Doors)

    Endpoints are the physical URLs where the frontend apps connect to the brain.

    Door 1: The Health Check

        @app.get("/")

        This is a simple entryway. When someone navigates to the root URL of the server, the get method returns a simple
         JSON message saying the API is live. It does not process any complex data.

    Door 2: The Main Chat Pipeline

        @app.post("/chat", response_model=SwarmResponse)

        This is the massive central nervous system of the app. It uses the @app.post decorator, meaning it expects
        the frontend to "post" (send) a package of data. The response_model=SwarmResponse tells FastAPI to guarantee
        the output will strictly match the SwarmResponse blueprint.

        When data enters this function as request: UserRequest, the data begins a complex flow:

        Step 1: Memory Retrieval (Database Fetch)
            The system checks if request.user_id already exists in the active_sessions RAM. If it does not, it means the user just opened the app.

            supabase.table("messages").select("*"): This command tells the database to select every column (*) from the "messages" drawer.

            .eq("user_id", request.user_id): This is the isolation wall. It filters the database, pulling only the messages
                                              where the user_id exactly matches the ID of the person asking the question.

            .order("created_at", desc=False): This sorts the messages by time so the conversation timeline remains chronological.

            .execute(): This is a crucial method. All the commands before this just prepare the SQL query. The .execute()
                        method is the trigger that actually fires the query across the internet to the Supabase servers and waits for the data to return.

            The system then loops through the past messages and loads them into the active_sessions RAM.

        Step 2: Committing the New Message

            The system appends the user's new question (request.prompt) to the RAM memory.

            Then, it permanently saves it to the database using .insert({...}).execute(). The .insert() method packages
            the data into a new database row. The .execute() method permanently writes it to the hard drive in the cloud.

        Step 3: Background Janitor

            The system checks if len(user_history) > 6:. If the conversation has more than 6 messages, it pauses, calls
            the compress_memory() worker to summarize the old messages, and replaces the heavy history with a lightweight "Fact Sheet."

        Step 4: The Manager Routing

            The system sends the question to get_manager_decision(). The manager returns a string (e.g., "RAG, WEB").
            The system creates an empty string called collected_context to hold the data it is about to find.

        Step 5: The Departments (Where the magic happens)

                The system uses if statements to check what the Manager decided.

            The RAG Department (Retrieval-Augmented Generation):

                  If the manager chose RAG, the system must search the private knowledge vault.

                It sends the user's English prompt to get_embedding to turn it into a math vector.

                It uses supabase.rpc('match_company_docs', {...}).execute(). RPC stands for "Remote Procedure Call."
                Instead of downloading the whole database to search it, rpc tells the Supabase server to run a custom SQL
                program locally on its own massive processors.

                Inside the RPC package, it passes query_embedding (the math), match_count: 1 (bring back the single best document),
                and p_user_id: request.user_id. Passing the request.user_id acts as a security badge; the SQL database will physically
                refuse to return any documents that do not belong to that exact user.

                If a document is found, its text is wrapped in <internal_company_data> XML tags and added to the collected_context.

            The WEB Department:

                If the manager chose WEB, the system calls get_search_query().

                The Elite Filter: The system checks if the micro-agent returned words like "NONE". any(phrase in optimized_query for
                                  phrase in forbidden_phrases) scans the output. If the query is valid, it calls perform_web_search()
                                   and wraps the live internet articles in <live_web_data> XML tags, adding them to the collected_context.

            The MATH Department:

                If the manager chose MATH, the system uses re.sub(r'[^0-9\+\-\*\/\(\)\.]', '', request.prompt).
                This regex method literally deletes every single character from the user's prompt except for numbers and
                 math symbols (like +, -, *, /). It passes the clean equation to calculate_math(), gets the answer, and
                 wraps it in <math_calculation> XML tags.

        Step 6: Final Synthesis and Return

            If any department collected data, the system creates a massive final_prompt. This prompt contains strict
            instructions ("You MUST address every single piece of data"), the XML data collected by the departments, and the original user question.

            It sends this massive package to send_to_cloud_ai().

            The AI generates a brilliant, highly informed answer (ai_words).

            The system uses .insert().execute() one last time to save the AI's answer into the Supabase database.

            Finally, it creates the SwarmResponse package, puts the answer inside it, and sends it back out the door to the user's screen.

    Door 3: The Knowledge Vault Upload

        @app.post("/upload-doc")

        This door is exclusively for saving files to the brain. The frontend sends the DocumentUpload package.

        Security Gate: The system looks at doc.admin_password. If it does not exactly match the secret environment variable,
                        the system returns an "error" status.

        Vectorization: It passes doc.content to get_embedding(). The Hugging Face AI turns the English document into the 384-dimensional math array.

        Storage: It calls supabase.table("company_docs").insert({...}).execute().

        Crucially, it inserts doc.user_id. By saving the user ID in the same row as the document, it builds the isolation wall.
        From this moment on, only that specific user ID can ever retrieve this specific document.

Part 6: The Engine Starter
    if __name__ == "__main__":
    This is the ignition switch. When a computer runs a Python file, it assigns the special name "__main__" to the file that was executed.

    port = int(os.environ.get("PORT", 8009)): Live cloud servers (like Render) dynamically assign network ports. This line asks the server,
                                             "What port do you want me to use?" and defaults to 8009 if it is running locally on a laptop.

    uvicorn.run("main:app", host="0.0.0.0", port=port): This commands the Uvicorn engine to start running the app. Setting host="0.0.0.0" tells
                                                        the server to open its gates and accept traffic from the entire public internet,
                                                        bringing the Enterprise Swarm API fully online.



Phase 1: Installing the Mathematical Brain

        SQL
            -- 1. Enable the vector extension
            create extension if not exists vector;

    What it means: A standard PostgreSQL database only understands text and numbers. It does not understand artificial
                   intelligence or multi-dimensional geometry. The word extension means a plugin. This line commands the
                   database to install a highly specialized plugin called pgvector. The if not exists part is a safety check:
                   it tells the database to only install it if it hasn't been installed already, preventing crashes.

    Connection to Python: In the Python code, the get_embedding() function uses Hugging Face to convert English words into a
                          list of 384 numbers. Without this vector extension enabled in SQL, the database would reject those
                          numbers, completely breaking the RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) department.

Phase 2: Building the Physical Filing Cabinet

    SQL

        -- 2. Create a table to hold your internal company documents
        create table company_docs (
          id bigserial primary key,
          content text,
          embedding vector(384) -- 384 is the exact size of the HuggingFace model we will use
        );

    create table company_docs: This tells the database to build a new spreadsheet (or filing cabinet) named company_docs.

    id bigserial primary key: Every piece of data needs a unique barcode so it can be found. bigserial means the database
                                will automatically generate a massive, sequential number (1, 2, 3...) for every new document.
                                primary key means this number is the official, unchangeable identity of that row.

    content text: This creates a column to hold an unlimited amount of raw English words.

    embedding vector(384): This creates a special column that can only hold an array of exactly 384 mathematical coordinates.

    Connection to Python: When a user uploads a document through the Python @app.post("/upload-doc") endpoint, the Python code
                          runs supabase.table("company_docs").insert(...). The database opens this exact company_docs table,
                          puts the English words into the content column, and puts the Hugging Face math into the embedding column.

